# secrets.env.example - template for secrets.env (copy, then edit the copy)
#
# WHAT THIS FILE STANDS IN FOR
#   On a real cloud, these values live in a secrets store (GCP Secret Manager, AWS Secrets Manager,
#   Azure Key Vault, a Kubernetes Secret). The platform reads them when the container starts and sets
#   them as environment variables. Here, docker compose reads secrets.env and does the same thing.
#   The app cannot tell the difference: it only reads environment variables.
#
# RULES
#   - This template is committed; it holds placeholders only.
#   - Your copy, secrets.env, holds real values and is NEVER committed (it is in .gitignore).
#   - On a real cloud there is no file: put each value in the secrets store and grant the service read access.
#
# PRE-PROD (test, staging)
#   Same compose file, same image; only the values differ.  On the pre-prod machine, its own secrets.env holds the
#   pre-prod database and key, and you start it with:  APILOGICPROJECT_ENV=preprod docker compose -f docker-compose-prod.yml up
#   (There is deliberately no docker-compose-test: one deployment definition, different values per environment.)
#
# FORMAT: NAME=value, one per line, no quotes, no spaces around =.

# SECRET_KEY - signs login tokens (SQL security). The server will not start in prod with the shipped placeholder.
#   Generate one:  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#   Left blank here on purpose: until you set it, the server refuses to start (try it).
SECRET_KEY=

# SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI - the prod database, including its password (which is why it is a secret).
#   host.docker.internal = your machine, as seen from inside the container (where the Postgres container's port is published).
#   On a real cloud: your managed database (Cloud SQL, RDS, Azure Database for PostgreSQL).
SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI=postgresql://postgres:p@host.docker.internal:5432/demomcpsendemail_prod
